1. What is Firmware?

Firmware is a special type of software that is stored inside hardware devices.

It tells the hardware how to work.

Think of it as the "operating instructions" for a hardware device.

Examples of hardware that has firmware:

BIOS/UEFI
RAID Controller
Network Card (NIC)
SSD/Hard Disk
Server Management Controller (iLO, iDRAC)

2. Real-Life Example

Think about a smart TV.

The TV has hardware like:

Screen
Speakers
Wi-Fi

But how does the TV know how to use them?

Because it has firmware stored inside it.

Sometimes the TV manufacturer releases a firmware update to:

Fix bugs
Improve Wi-Fi performance
Add new features

You install the update.

The hardware now works better.

The same idea applies to servers.

3. Firmware vs Operating System

Many beginners confuse these two.

| Firmware               | Operating System        |
| ---------------------- | ----------------------- |
| Stored inside hardware | Installed on disk       |
| Controls hardware      | Runs applications       |
| Starts before the OS   | Starts after firmware   |
| Example: BIOS          | Example: Linux, Solaris |

4. Boot Process
Power ON
     │
     ▼
Firmware (BIOS/UEFI)
     │
     ▼
Bootloader
     │
     ▼
Linux / Solaris
     │
     ▼
Applications

Firmware starts first and prepares the hardware so the operating system can boot.


